WEBVTT

NOTE
Automatic English captions. Generated locally and checked with additional audio decodes; not fully audited by listening.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.100
Tables become much more useful when
workflows can act on them directly. Maybe

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.440
you need to find every new lead, add a row
when something happens, update a status

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:15.200
after an agent finishes, or clean up
records you no longer need. The table

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.020
block is what connects those structured
records to the rest of your workload, so

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:24.460
data can move in and out of tables as the
process runs. Let me show you how the

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:25.480
table block works.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.980
The table block works across two surfaces.
A panel where you describe the operation

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:33.960
and the live table that answers it.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.840
Everything the block can do to a table
starts from the operation dropdown, and it

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.900
opens on query rows.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:47.940
A query is shaped by its filter, so we
build one condition, priority equals high.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:56.980
Run it, and three rows match. The table
itself is untouched because a query only

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:57.980
reads.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:03.380
Now change just the operator to not
equals, and the same run selects the

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:04.380
opposite rows.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:12.020
Conditions stack, so adding status equals
open narrows the match to a single ticket.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.240
Delete rows by filter takes the same
shape, one filter, and the matching row

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:21.240
leaves the table.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:28.520
Insert row swaps the filter for row data,
and every key in the payload lands as a

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:29.600
cell in the new row.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.060
Update rows by filter combines both. The
filter picks the open tickets, and the row

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.880
data flips each one to resolved.

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:47.800
Each operation follows the same shape. You
describe it in the panel, run the block,

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:49.220
and read the result in the table.
